💡 **SUGGESTION: probe failure is rethrown as "Branch was not successfully pushed to remote", masking the real cause**

The gh-912 migration wrapped the push-verify probe in a try/catch:

```js
} catch (lsRemoteErr) {
    console.warn('Error checking remote branches:', lsRemoteErr);
}
if (!remoteBranchExists) {
    throw new Error('Branch was not successfully pushed to remote');
}
```

Behavior change vs the old code: previously a probe failure propagated the raw `ls-remote` error; now a network blip **after a successful push** is reported as "branch was not pushed", which will send the operator looking at the wrong thing (the branch *is* on the remote; the probe failed). The true cause only reaches `console.warn`.

Suggest preserving the cause in the thrown message (the same pattern appears in `postStoryTestAutomationResults.js`, `postTestAutomationResults.js` second probe, and `storyTestAutomationRework.js`):

```js
throw new Error('Branch was not successfully pushed to remote' +
    (lsRemoteErr ? ' (verify probe failed: ' + (lsRemoteErr.message || lsRemoteErr) + ')' : ''));
```

Failure outcome is identical either way and the warn log retains the detail, so this is diagnosability polish only.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `PR_HELPER_REAL_GH` is referenced before its assignment**

```js
// line 22 (inside loadGithubHelpers):
'./pullRequest.js': PR_HELPER_REAL_GH,
...
// line 31 (after the loader definition):
var PR_HELPER_REAL_GH = loadModule('js/common/pullRequest.js', ...);
```

This works only because `loadGithubHelpers` is invoked from `test()` callbacks after module evaluation finishes, by which time the `var` assignment has run — but it reads like a TDZ/`undefined` bug and is the only test file in this PR with the definition *after* the loader (`test_workingDir.js`, `test_pushReworkChanges.js`, `test_reworkCustomParams.js`, `test_preCliSnapshotSetup.js` all define it first). Moving the `var PR_HELPER_REAL_GH = ...` declaration above `loadGithubHelpers` removes the trap for the next reader. Cosmetic.

